### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?

Follow-up to #12494. 57811ef0 gave the [adoption agency algorithm](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/parsing.html#adoption-agency-algorithm)'s insertion of `lastNode` into the common ancestor an explicit guard, but three other moves in the same algorithm are still unguarded, and each directly follows a *create an element for the token* that can run author script:

1. In the inner loop, "Append `lastNode` to `node`", immediately after "Create an element for the token for which the element `node` was created, in the HTML namespace, with `commonAncestor` as the intended parent … and let `node` be the new element".
2. "Take all of the child nodes of `furthestBlock` and append them to the element created in the last step."
3. "Append that new element to `furthestBlock`."

*Create an element for the token* runs a custom element constructor and invokes `attributeChangedCallback` when the token's attributes are appended, both before the element is returned. Since `node` and `formattingElement` are always in the list of active formatting elements, their local names are one of `a, b, big, code, em, font, i, nobr, s, small, strike, strong, tt, u` — never a valid custom element name — so only a customized built-in element reaches these steps. Once it does, the reaction has `this` and can move it into the very subtree the following step then tries to move into it. *Append* is the checked DOM operation, so it throws a `HierarchyRequestError` out of an algorithm with nowhere to propagate it.

Reduction for (1):

```html
<script>
customElements.define('x-i', class extends HTMLElement {
  static get observedAttributes() { return ['x']; }
  attributeChangedCallback() {
    window.n = (window.n || 0) + 1;
    if (window.n === 2)
      document.getElementById('fb').appendChild(this);
  }
}, { extends: 'i' });
</script>
<body><b><i is="x-i" x="1"><p id=fb>x</b>
```

On `</b>`, `formattingElement` is `b`, `furthestBlock` is `p#fb`, and `commonAncestor` is `body`. The first inner-loop iteration takes `node` = `i`, which is in the list of active formatting elements, so a fresh `i` is created from the saved token — invoking `attributeChangedCallback` a second time, which appends that new element into `#fb`. `lastNode` is still `furthestBlock`, i.e. `#fb`, so "Append `lastNode` to `node`" tries to insert `#fb` into an element whose parent is `#fb`: `#fb` is a host-including inclusive ancestor of the parent, and *ensure pre-insertion validity* throws.

The other two are reachable the same way, from the reaction for `formattingElement`'s recreated element:

- (2) `furthestBlock.appendChild(this)` makes the new element one of `furthestBlock`'s children, so "take all of the child nodes of `furthestBlock` and append them to the element created in the last step" appends that element to itself.
- (3) `this.appendChild(furthestBlock)` makes `furthestBlock` a child of the new element, so "append that new element to `furthestBlock`" appends it below itself.
